new_candidates.body:
An SDFormat `<joint>` whose `<parent>` or `<child>` names a frame of a static
model with no links makes the parser throw an unrelated exception from
`GetRigidBodyByName()`. It names neither the joint nor the file location.
libsdformat reports no error. It doesn't matter whether the joint names an
explicit frame of the model (`s::F`) or the model's own implicit frame (`s`).

Observed on master at 9726576eb6 (Ubuntu 24.04, Bazel opt build).

**Repro.** This program compiles as is against Drake.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

#include <fmt/format.h>

#include "drake/multibody/parsing/parser.h"
#include "drake/multibody/plant/multibody_plant.h"

using drake::multibody::MultibodyPlant;
using drake::multibody::Parser;

int main() {
  // A static model 's' with no links, only a frame, and a joint that names a
  // frame of 's' as its <parent> or <child>.
  for (const char* parent_and_child :
       {"<parent>s::F</parent><child>D</child>",
        "<parent>s</parent><child>D</child>",
        "<parent>D</parent><child>s::F</child>"}) {
    MultibodyPlant<double> plant(0.0);
    std::cout << parent_and_child << "\n";
    try {
      Parser(&plant).AddModelsFromString(
          fmt::format(R"(<sdf version='1.12'>
  <model name='m'>
    <model name='s'>
      <static>true</static>
      <frame name='F'/>
    </model>
    <link name='D'/>
    <joint name='j' type='revolute'>
      {}
      <axis><xyz>0 0 1</xyz></axis>
    </joint>
  </model>
</sdf>)",
                      parent_and_child),
          "sdf");
      std::cout << "  parsed\n";
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
      std::cout << "  threw: " << e.what() << "\n";
    }
  }
}
```

**Output:**

```
<parent>s::F</parent><child>D</child>
  threw: GetRigidBodyByName(): There is no RigidBody named '__model__' anywhere in the model (valid names in model instance 'WorldModelInstance' are: world; valid names in model instance 'm' are: D)
<parent>s</parent><child>D</child>
  threw: GetRigidBodyByName(): There is no RigidBody named '__model__' anywhere in the model (valid names in model instance 'WorldModelInstance' are: world; valid names in model instance 'm' are: D)
<parent>D</parent><child>s::F</child>
  threw: GetRigidBodyByName(): There is no RigidBody named '__model__' anywhere in the model (valid names in model instance 'WorldModelInstance' are: world; valid names in model instance 'm' are: D)
```

**Expected:** at least a parse error that names the joint and its location.
For `<parent>`, a joint to the world at that frame might be better, since a
static model's frames are fixed to the world. A `<drake:joint>` can already do
that: since #25132, `<drake:parent>s::F</drake:parent>` works and gives a
joint frame on the world.

**Cause:** `AddJointFromSpecification()` looks up the joint's links by the
names that `sdf::Joint::ResolveParentLink()` and `ResolveChildLink()` return
([detail_sdf_parser.cc#L862-L866](https://github.com/RobotLocomotion/drake/blob/9726576eb689de7582ed81c4c6b8319aef0ae3da/multibody/parsing/detail_sdf_parser.cc#L862-L866)).
For a frame of a model with no links, the resolved name is the model's
`__model__` frame rather than a link. `GetBodyByLinkSpecificationName()` then
throws while looking it up as a body.

**Interaction with #25125:** a `<joint>` with `<drake:independent_frames/>`
must not name these frames in either `<parent>` or `<child>`. Doing so is a
located parse error, and a TODO there refers to this issue.
